+# Contract: `logredact` command-line interface (v2)
+
+**Feature**: 002-phone-redaction-out-dir | **Date**: 2026-11-21
+
+Supersedes [../../001-log-data-redaction-cli/contracts/cli.md](../../001-log-data-redaction-cli/contracts/cli.md)
+for the sections marked **changed** below. Everything else in that document stands unchanged and
+remains binding (FR-005). Recognition rules live in [redaction-rules.md](./redaction-rules.md).
+
+---
+
+## Invocation *(changed)*
+
+```text
+python -m logredact [--count] [--out-dir DIR] [FILE ...]
+```
+
+Requires `src/` on the module search path (FR-018):
+
+```bash
+PYTHONPATH=src python -m logredact --count --out-dir clean app.log web.log
+```
+
+**Package location**: `src/logredact/`. **Interpreter**: CPython 3.11 or newer. **Runtime imports**:
+Python standard library only (FR-018, Constitution Principle I). **Platform**: POSIX — Linux or
+macOS — because `--out-dir`'s link and permission controls are implemented with POSIX `open` flags
+and `fchmod` (research.md D-113).
+
+---
+
+## Arguments *(changed)*
+
+| Argument | Form | Cardinality | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `--count` | option, no value | 0 or 1 | also write a tally to stderr |
+| `--out-dir` | option, one value | 0 or more | write one redacted file per FILE into `DIR` |
+| `FILE` | positional | 0..n | input path, consumed in the order given |
+
+**Rules**
+
+- Both options are accepted in **any position** among the arguments (FR-006):
+  `--out-dir clean a.log`, `a.log --out-dir clean`, and `a.log --out-dir clean b.log` are
+  equivalent, and `clean` is never treated as an input path.
+- `--out-dir DIR` and `--out-dir=DIR` are both accepted (FR-006).
+- `--out-dir` repeated uses the **last** occurrence (FR-006).
+- `--out-dir` with no value following it is a usage error: exit 2 (FR-006).
+- `--out-dir ""` is a usage error: exit 2 (SC-008, SR-012).
+- `--out-dir` with **no FILE** is a usage error: exit 2. Standard input is **not** read and no
+  directory or file is created. There is no fallback to stdout and no fallback to stdin (FR-012).
+- Every non-option argument is a FILE path, in order. Repetition is allowed and the file is read —
+  and written — once per occurrence (FR-011).
+- A FILE of `-` is a **literal relative path**, not a stdin alias.
+- Input paths are used verbatim: not resolved, not normalised, not restricted to a base directory
+  (001 SR-009). **Output** paths are not: see *Output folder* below (SR-005).
+- `-h` / `--help` prints usage — now documenting `--out-dir` — to **stdout** and exits **0**.
+- There is no option, environment variable, or configuration file that disables or weakens any of
+  the four redaction categories (SR-003).
+
+---
+
+## Input *(unchanged)*
+
+| Condition | Source read |
+|---|---|
+| no FILE arguments, no `--out-dir` | standard input |
+| no FILE arguments, `--out-dir` given | **nothing is read** — usage error, exit 2 (FR-012) |
+| one or more FILE arguments | each FILE in argument order; stdin is not read at all |
+
+Encoding `utf-8` with `errors="surrogateescape"`; newlines never translated; reads bounded to
+**1 MiB (1 048 576 characters)** of decoded content per line; files opened read-only and closed
+before the next is opened (FR-005, SR-011).
+
+---
+
+## Destinations *(changed)*
+
+A run has exactly one destination for its redacted stream.
+
+| `--out-dir` | Redacted lines go to | stdout also carries | Created on disk |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| absent | standard output | nothing | **nothing at all** (FR-017, SR-008) |
+| given | one file per FILE inside DIR | only `wrote N files` | DIR, its missing parents, the output files |
+
+### Standard output
+
+- **Without `--out-dir`** — unchanged from 001: every input line in input order with redactions
+  applied; all non-matching characters and the original line structure preserved exactly; no
+  trailing newline added to input whose final line lacked one; empty input gives empty stdout; the
+  summary never appears here; flushed before success is reported.
+- **With `--out-dir`** — stdout carries **exactly one line**, `wrote N files`, and nothing else. No
+  redacted content reaches stdout (FR-007, FR-010, US2-2).
+
+### Output folder (`--out-dir DIR`)
+
+| # | Rule | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| O-1 | DIR is created if absent, together with every missing parent, **before** any output file is opened | FR-008 |
+| O-2 | Each directory component the tool creates is created with owner-only access, `0o700` | SR-007, SC-007 |
+| O-3 | A directory that already exists is used as-is; its mode is not changed and its unrelated files are left untouched | SR-008 |
+| O-4 | The DIR value itself is used **as given** — not resolved, not rebased | Assumptions |
+| O-5 | One output file per FILE argument, named by the **final path component of that FILE only**. The FILE's directory components, `..` segments, and absolute root are discarded and never influence the location | FR-007, SR-005 |
+| O-6 | The joined path is resolved and must be a **direct child** of the resolved DIR, or the run fails | SR-005 |
+| O-7 | A final path component that is empty, `.`, or `..` is a loud failure, not a guess | SR-005 |
+| O-8 | An existing regular file of that name is **replaced in full** — truncated, never appended, no remnant of longer previous content | FR-009 |
+| O-9 | An existing path that is **not a regular file** — symlink, directory, device, socket, FIFO — is never followed or written; the run fails | SR-006 |
+| O-10 | A path that refers to the **same file as an input FILE** is refused before any truncation; the input is never truncated | SR-008 |
+| O-11 | Output files are created, and existing ones left, with owner-only read and write, `0o600` | SR-007, SC-007 |
+| O-12 | Output files use the identical encoding and newline policy as stdout, so non-UTF-8 bytes reach them unchanged, newline sequences are neither translated nor normalised, and an unterminated final line gains no terminator | FR-013 |
+| O-13 | At most **one** output file is open at any instant: opened after its FILE's input stream, closed before the next FILE is reached | SR-011 |
+| O-14 | Nothing is written to any path other than DIR, its missing parents, and these output files. No temporary file is created | SR-008, SR-007 |
+
+### The tally line
+
+```text
+wrote N files
+```
+
+- Written to **standard output**, terminated by `\n`, exactly once, only with `--out-dir`
+  (FR-010).
+- `N` is the number of FILE arguments processed. A FILE named twice counts twice; two FILEs sharing
+  a final path component count twice and the later output replaces the earlier (FR-011).
+- For exactly one file the line reads `wrote 1 file` — singular (Assumptions).
+- Written **after** every output file has been written, flushed, and closed successfully, and
+  **before** the `--count` summary (FR-010, FR-016).
+- Never written on a failing run (FR-014, FR-015, US4-1).
+
+---
+
+## Standard error *(changed)*
+
+| Run | stderr content |
+|---|---|
+| success, no `--count` | **empty** — with or without `--out-dir` (FR-005, US3-6) |
+| success, `--count` | exactly one line: `emails=N ips=N cards=N phones=N\n` |
+| failure | one diagnostic line; **no summary**, and no tally on stdout (US4-8) |
+
+### Summary format (`--count` only) *(changed — supersedes 001 FR-010)*
+
+```text
+emails=N ips=N cards=N phones=N
+```
+
+- Exactly that form: lowercase keys in that order, single `=`, single spaces, terminated by `\n`,
+  no leading or trailing padding (FR-004).
+- Each `N` is the **total number of replacements** of that category across all input — not lines
+  affected, not distinct values. The same number three times counts three (FR-004, US3-3).
+- `phones` is **appended**, not inserted, so a script reading the first three fields positionally
+  keeps working (Assumptions).
+- Written only on a successful run, after all redacted output has been written and flushed; with
+  `--out-dir`, after every output file is complete **and** after the `wrote N files` line
+  (FR-004, FR-016, US3-5).
+- With no redactable input: `emails=0 ips=0 cards=0 phones=0` (US3-2).
+
+### Diagnostics *(changed — new conditions added)*
+
+One line to stderr, naming the problem. A diagnostic **must not** contain any input line content,
+matched value, or fragment of one. The only input-derived strings permitted are paths the invoking
+user supplied on the command line — a FILE path, the DIR value, or an output path derived from them
+(SR-001). A stack trace is never printed (SR-001).
+
+| Condition | Message shape | Usage text? | Status |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| FILE does not exist / permission denied / is a directory | names the path, reports it cannot be read | no | 2 |
+| line exceeds 1 MiB | names the input source, states the limit, **does not quote the line** | no | 2 |
+| stdout write/flush fails | reports the write failure | no | 2 |
+| unrecognised option | `argparse` usage message | yes | 2 |
+| `--out-dir` given with no FILE | reports that `--out-dir` requires at least one FILE | yes | 2 |
+| `--out-dir` value empty | reports that DIR must not be empty | yes | 2 |
+| `--out-dir` value missing | `argparse`: `expected one argument` | yes | 2 |
+| DIR cannot be created / exists as a regular file / a component is not a directory | names DIR | no | 2 |
+| FILE has no usable final path component (`""`, `.`, `..`) | names the FILE and DIR | no | 2 |
+| output path is not a direct child of the resolved DIR | names the output path | no | 2 |
+| output path exists and is not a regular file (symlink, directory, device, socket, FIFO) | names the output path, reports it is not a regular file | no | 2 |
+| output path is the same file as an input FILE | names both paths, reports the refusal | no | 2 |
+| output file open / write / flush / close fails | names the output path | no | 2 |
+
+---
+
+## Exit status *(unchanged)*
+
+| Status | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| **0** | all input was read, and all output was written, flushed, and closed successfully |
+| **2** | any failure: unreadable FILE, over-long line, write failure, invalid usage, or any output-folder failure above |
+
+Exit status 0 is produced **only** when the full redacted stream reached its destination. A
+truncated or missing output file is never reported as success (FR-014, FR-015, SR-009, SC-008).
+
+---
+
+## Ordering and failure semantics *(changed)*
+
+- Argument validation happens first, before stdin is read, before any FILE is opened, and before
+  DIR is touched (SR-012).
+- DIR is created once, after validation and before the first FILE is opened (FR-008).
+- FILEs are processed in argument order and streamed as they are read. With `--out-dir`, output
+  file *n* is written, flushed, and closed before FILE *n+1* is opened (O-13).
+- FILEs are **not** pre-validated. An unreadable FILE is reported the moment it is reached:
+  output files already completed are **left in place**, later FILEs are not opened, and neither the
+  tally nor the summary is written (FR-014, US4-1).
+- A failed write may leave a **partial** output file on disk. Exit status 2, the diagnostic, and the
+  absent `wrote N files` line are the signal that the bundle is incomplete; there is no
+  write-to-temporary-and-rename step (SR-009, Assumptions).
+
+---
+
+## Guarantees *(changed)*
+
+| ID | Guarantee |
+|---|---|
+| G-1 | Input is inert data: never evaluated, deserialised, interpolated, or executed; no shell or subprocess is invoked (001 SR-004) |
+| G-2 | Placeholders are inserted literally; template or backreference syntax in the input cannot alter a substitution (001 SR-004) |
+| G-3 | **Without `--out-dir`**: no file, directory, temporary file, or cache is created, written, or modified anywhere (FR-017, SR-008) |
+| G-3b | **With `--out-dir`**: the only paths created or written are DIR, its missing parents, and one regular file per FILE directly inside DIR. No FILE value can direct a write outside DIR (SR-005, SR-008) |
+| G-4 | Input files are opened read-only, never for writing, creation, truncation, or appending (SR-008) |
+| G-5 | No network access of any kind is performed |
+| G-6 | Matching is linear in line length; no nested unbounded repetition exists in any pattern (SR-004) |
+| G-7 | A whole recognised value is replaced, never a prefix or suffix, in all four categories (SR-002) |
+| G-8 | The bytes in an output file are exactly the redacted stream for that FILE; no original input, pre-redaction buffer, or matched value is written to any file (SR-010) |
+
+---
+
+## Internal entry point (for tests) *(changed)*
+
+Not part of the user-facing contract, but fixed here so tests can bind to it:
+
+```python
+def main(argv: list[str], *, stdin=None, stdout=None, stderr=None) -> int: ...
+```
+
+Returns the exit status instead of raising `SystemExit`; streams default to the real `sys` streams
+when omitted. `__main__.py` is the thin shell that calls it and exits with its return value.
+
+Two output-folder helpers in `cli` are also fixed here, because they carry security requirements
+that deserve direct tests rather than only end-to-end coverage (SR-005, SR-006, SR-007):
+
+```python
+def output_path_for(out_dir: str, file_path: str) -> str: ...   # SR-005 derivation + containment
+def open_output(path: str, input_fd: int): ...                  # SR-006/SR-007/SR-008 safe open
+```
+
+Both raise `OSError` (or `ValueError` for an unusable final path component) rather than reporting;
+`main` owns every diagnostic and every exit status.
