+# Contract: redaction recognition rules (four ranks)
+
+**Feature**: 002-phone-redaction-out-dir | **Date**: 2026-11-21
+
+This document is authoritative for the **phone** rule and for the **four-rank precedence**.
+Ranks 1–3 are defined by
+[../../001-log-data-redaction-cli/contracts/redaction-rules.md](../../001-log-data-redaction-cli/contracts/redaction-rules.md)
+and are **unchanged, character for character**; this feature neither widens nor narrows them.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Ranks 1–3 — unchanged
+
+| Rank | Category | Placeholder | Definition |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | email | `[EMAIL]` | 001 contract §1 (FR-004) |
+| 2 | IPv4 | `[IP]` | 001 contract §2 (FR-005) |
+| 3 | payment card | `[CARD]` | 001 contract §3 (FR-006, FR-007) |
+
+---
+
+## 2. Rank 4 — phone → `[PHONE]` (FR-001, FR-002)
+
+### Grammar
+
+```text
+phone ::= "+" ASCII-DIGIT{8,15}       not immediately followed by ASCII-DIGIT
+ASCII-DIGIT ::= "0" | "1" | ... | "9"
+```
+
+As implemented:
+
+```python
+r"\+[0-9]{8,15}(?![0-9])"
+```
+
+### Rules
+
+| # | Rule |
+|---|---|
+| P-1 | The match begins at a literal `+`. The character **before** the `+` is never examined, so a `+` after a digit, a letter, another `+`, or at line start all anchor equally. |
+| P-2 | Between 8 and 15 ASCII digits must follow the `+` **immediately and contiguously**. Any intervening character — space, hyphen, dot, parenthesis — ends the candidate. |
+| P-3 | Digits are ASCII `0`–`9` only. Non-ASCII decimal digit characters (for example Arabic-Indic `٠`–`٩`) do not count and do not qualify. |
+| P-4 | The longest qualifying length is taken. Because of P-5 exactly one length can qualify at a given `+`, so longest and leftmost never conflict. |
+| P-5 | The final digit of the match must **not** be immediately followed by another ASCII digit. A `+` followed by 16 or more contiguous digits therefore matches **nothing at all** — it does not yield `[PHONE]` plus the surplus digits (SR-002). |
+| P-6 | Matching is line-scoped. A number split across a line terminator is not recognised, as for every other category. |
+| P-7 | The whole recognised span, `+` included, is replaced by the literal text `[PHONE]`. |
+
+### Worked cases
+
+All rows below were executed against the implementation design before this contract was written
+(research.md D-101, D-102).
+
+| Input | Output | Rule |
+|---|---|---|
+| `call +14155552671 now` | `call [PHONE] now` | P-2, P-7 |
+| `+12345678` | `[PHONE]` | P-2, lower bound |
+| `+123456789012345` | `[PHONE]` | P-2, upper bound |
+| `+1234567` | `+1234567` (unchanged) | P-2, 7 digits |
+| `+1234567890123456` | unchanged | **P-5**, 16 digits |
+| `+ 14155552671` | unchanged | P-2, digit not adjacent |
+| `+1 415 555 2671` | unchanged | P-2, digits not contiguous |
+| `+١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨` | unchanged | P-3 |
+| `+` | unchanged | P-2 |
+| `++14155552671` | `+[PHONE]` | P-1, the second `+` anchors |
+| `100+14155552671` | `100[PHONE]` | P-1, left context ignored |
+| `+14155552671.` | `[PHONE].` | P-5 satisfied by a non-digit |
+| `+14155552671 +442071838750` | `[PHONE] [PHONE]` | every occurrence replaced |
+
+---
+
+## 3. Precedence and overlap (FR-003)
+
+### The four ranks
+
+```text
+email  (1)  >  IPv4  (2)  >  card  (3)  >  phone  (4)
+```
+
+### Rules
+
+| # | Rule |
+|---|---|
+| R-1 | A span claimed by a higher-ranked rule is **never** offered to a lower-ranked one. |
+| R-2 | Each character of a line is replaced at most once. Placeholders are never nested or doubly substituted. |
+| R-3 | Inserted placeholder text is never re-scanned: a literal `[PHONE]` in the input stays exactly that. |
+| R-4 | Consequently **every input the tool redacts today is still redacted into the same placeholder at the same position** (FR-003, FR-005). Phone being last is what makes this literally true. |
+
+### Mechanism
+
+Three stages over one line, each offered only what the previous stages left uncovered
+(research.md D-102):
+
+```text
+stage 1   EMAIL_OR_IP.finditer(line)              -> ranks 1 and 2, in alternation order
+stage 2   find_card_spans(slice)  for each gap    -> rank 3, over the gaps of stage 1
+stage 3   _PHONE.finditer(slice)  for each gap    -> rank 4, over the gaps of stages 1 and 2
+rebuild   verbatim input slices + literal placeholder constants
+```
+
+Rank 4 must be a separate stage rather than a fourth alternative in the stage-1 pattern: the card
+rule is not expressible as a regex (its Luhn checksum is computed in Python), so no single
+alternation can place phone *below* card.
+
+**Slice-boundary soundness.** Rule P-5's lookahead is evaluated at the end of a gap slice rather
+than of the whole line, which would be unsound if a gap could end part-way through a digit run. It
+cannot: a gap ends where a rank 1–3 match begins, and no rank 1–3 match can begin with a digit
+immediately preceded by a digit — email carries `(?<![A-Za-z0-9._%+-])`, IPv4 carries `(?<![0-9.])`,
+and a card span can only start at the beginning of a maximal digit run.
+
+### Overlap cases
+
+| Input | Output | Why |
+|---|---|---|
+| `+14155552671@example.com` | `[EMAIL]` | `+` and the digits are a valid email local part; rank 1 beats rank 4 |
+| `+10.0.0.7` | `+[IP]` | the digit run after `+` is interrupted by `.` before 8 are reached; rank 2 claims the address |
+| `+4222222222222` | `+[CARD]` | 13 digits passing Luhn; **rank 3 beats rank 4**, so nothing redacted today changes category |
+| `4111111111111111+14155552671` | `[CARD][PHONE]` | adjacent spans of different ranks, each replaced once |
+| `[PHONE] +14155552671` | `[PHONE] [PHONE]` | the literal input text is untouched (R-3); the real number is replaced |
+| `user jane@example.com from 10.0.0.7 card 4111 1111 1111 1111` | `user [EMAIL] from [IP] card [CARD]` | byte-for-byte what 001 produced (FR-005) |
+
+---
+
+## 4. Complexity guarantee (SR-004)
+
+| Property | Phone rule |
+|---|---|
+| quantifier | `{8,15}` — bounded, no nesting, no alternation inside a repetition |
+| worst case per `+` | ≤ 8 bounded attempts (15 down to 8 digits) before the position is abandoned |
+| overall | linear in line length |
+
+Measured on 10 000-character adversarial single lines through the complete four-rank pipeline,
+against SC-010's 1 s budget: a `+` run 1.53 ms, a digit run 0.67 ms, an alternating `+1` run
+6.78 ms, a `+`+16-digit run (worst backtracking case) 3.10 ms. Smallest margin: 147×.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Placeholders
+
+| Category | Placeholder | Properties |
+|---|---|---|
+| email | `[EMAIL]` | fixed literal |
+| IPv4 | `[IP]` | fixed literal |
+| card | `[CARD]` | fixed literal |
+| phone | `[PHONE]` | fixed literal |
+
+All four are non-reversible and carry **no length hint, padding, hash, or counter**, so no input
+value is recoverable from the output (SR-001). They are inserted as literal strings, never as a
+substitution template, so backreference or template syntax in the input (`\1`, `\g<0>`, `${x}`)
+cannot alter a substitution (001 SR-004, G-2).
+
+---
+
+## 6. Counts
+
+One increment per replacement, per category, in a `dict[str, int]` with keys `emails`, `ips`,
+`cards`, `phones`. Reported only by `--count`, only on stderr, only on a successful run — see
+[cli.md](./cli.md).
